1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
AI agents already pay invoices, and an agent that pays can be fooled:

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,500
a hidden instruction, a look-alike domain, an amount that does not fit its task.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:14,000
SafePay is the firewall every agent asks before it pays with PayPal: for teams
that put agents on their invoices, and for platforms that give agents a wallet.

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:20,000
The rules are written once, in plain words. Nemotron turns them into a policy
and shows me every field.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
I apply it signed with a key this browser created and that never leaves it.
Nobody changes these rules without that key, not even with server access.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A real Notion invoice: policy in order, vendor checked on the live web,
and the reasoning confirms it serves the task. Paid through the PayPal sandbox.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The same invoice to a look-alike domain: the vendor directory blocks it
without calling the model.

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Nine hundred dollars: over the per-payment cap. Hard block.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:01,000
One hundred and twenty dollars to a casino, inside every limit, and still held:
the web found complaints and the payment does not serve the task.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
A spending cap never catches this.

11
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:09,000
A legitimate invoice, but over the three hundred I asked to be consulted on.
It waits for me.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,500
One tap and it is paid. The approval is signed by this browser's key:
a stolen operator token approves nothing.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The last one is blocked because it would breach the daily cap across the burst:
SafePay keeps the day's count.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,500
If something smells wrong, one click freezes the agent the way a bank freezes a card.
A PayPal dispute pulls the same switch on its own.

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Every decision is a link in a signed hash chain.
I tamper with one row and the whole chain fails at once.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
We measured SafePay against seventeen real attacks.
But a published benchmark can be studied,

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
so here is one that cannot: the model is writing six attacks it has never seen,
right now, and SafePay judges them live.

18
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Four of six. Two got through, shown as they are.
Every miss comes with the rule that would close it.

19
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
One click signs those rules with my key, applies them,
and runs the same attacks again.

20
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,500
Six of six. Miss, fix and catch, in front of you.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,500
The same gate sits in front of PayPal's Agent Toolkit: the order is created only after
SafePay allows it, and the buyer approval step PayPal requires stays intact.

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
And any agent can ask before its own checkout over MCP and get a signed verdict;
the same rules apply to PYUSD and USDC.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
This already runs inside Impulsa, our SaaS for micro-businesses in Chile: every supplier
invoice goes through SafePay, and what each owner does with the alert is measured and published.

24
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
SafePay: rules in plain words, vendor checked, reasoning about every payment,
signed approvals and a record nobody edits. pip install safepay. Thank you.
